          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            The
            %em monoidal Dold-Kan correspondence
            relates
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+algebra"} simplicial algebra
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+graded+algebra"} differential graded algebra
          %p
            There is a plain
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dold-Kan+correspondence"} Dold-Kan correspondence
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+group"} simplicial group
            = succeed "es." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complex"} chain complex
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            structures. It turns out that the Dold-Kan correspondence does respect this monoidal structure, to some extent strictly, but generally in the sense of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+category+theory"} higher category theory
          %p
            This way it extends to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+equivalence"} Quillen equivalence
            between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+categories"} model categories
            of
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoids"} monoids
            in simplicial groups –
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+ring"} simplicial ring
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-algebra"} dg-algebra
          %p Notice that
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                monoids in the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+chain+complexes"} category of chain complexes
                are
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+graded+rings"} differential graded rings
            %li
              %p
                monoids in the category of simplicial abelian groups are
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+object"} simplicial
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rings"} rings
          %p
            If instead we look at chain complexes and
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+object"} simplicial object
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ab"} Ab
            but in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vect"} Vect
            then
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                monoids in the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+chain+complexes"} category of chain complexes
                of vector spaces are
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+graded+algebras"} differential graded algebras
            %li
              %p monoids in the category of simplicial vector spaces are simplicial algebras.
          %p
            Analogous statements apply to the dual Dold-Kan correspondence, where the monoids in question are accordingly cosimplicial rings and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+graded+algebras"} differential graded algebras
            with differential of positive degree.
          %p
            A crucial fact about the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dold-Kan+correspondence"} Dold-Kan correspondence
            is that
          %ul
            %li
              The two functors in the Dold-Kan correspondence individually respect these monoidal structures, in the sense that they are lax
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+functors"} monoidal functors
              (but not
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+monoidal+functors"} strong monoidal functors
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#RemarkOnLaxStrongDistinction"}
          %p
            However, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjunction"} adjunction
            fails to be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+adjunction"} monoidal adjunction
            of any sort (i.e. the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unit+of+an+adjunction"} unit
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/counit+of+an+adjunction"} counit
            are not monoidal natural transformations). (Note that if it were a monoidal adjunction, then by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/doctrinal+adjunction"} doctrinal adjunction
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence of categories
            or even an adjunction between (co)simplicial rings and (co)chain complexes.
          %p There are two different versions of the monoidal Dold-Kan corespondence, which are almost but apparently not entirely formal duals of each other (at least not in the detailed constructions):
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the
                %strong simplicial version
                that relates
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
                = succeed "s" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+group"} simplicial group
                = succeed "es" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complex"} chain complex
                = succeed "s)," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-algebra"} dg-algebra
            %li
              %p
                the
                %strong cosimplicial version
                that relates monoids in cosimplicial groups (
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosimplicial+algebra"}> cosimplicial ring
                ) to monoidal in co-chain complexes (cochain
                = succeed "s)." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-algebra"} dg-algebra
          %p
            The cosimplicial version is made monoidal by replacing the the Moore complex functor by something else, to obtain a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+equivalence"} Quillen equivalence
          %h2#Summary Summary of Quillen equivalences
          %p
            Below we discuss the following
