          %h2#simplicial Simplicial algebras and chain dg-algebras
          %p
            We first discuss the extent to which the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Moore+complex"} Moore complex
            functor is monoidal. Then we use this to discuss various Quillen equivalences on model categories of monoids that it induces.
          %h3#BilaxFrobStruc Bilax monoidal Frobenius structure on normalized chains
          #ChainsIsLaxMonoidal.num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Moore+complex"} Moore complex
              functor
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi sAb
                    %mo →
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Ch
                      %mo •
                      %mo +
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    C_\bullet \colon sAb \to Ch_\bullet^+
            %p as well as the normalized chains/normalized Moore complex functor
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi N
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi sAb
                    %mo →
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Ch
                      %mo •
                      %mo +
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    N_\bullet \colon sAb \to Ch_\bullet^+
            %p are both
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+functor"} symmetric
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \;
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax+monoidal+functors"} lax monoidal functors
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/oplax+monoidal+functors"} oplax monoidal functors
                %p
                  (but
                  %em not
                  oplax
                  %em symmetric
                  monoidal)
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bilax+monoidal+functors"} bilax monoidal functors
              %li
                %p
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frobenius+monoidal+functors"}> Frobenius monoidal functors
                  \.
            %p
              The lax structure is given by the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-Zilber+map"} Eilenberg-Zilber map
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∇
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %mo ,
                        %mi B
                    %mo :
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\nabla_{A,B} : N(A) \otimes N(B) \to N(A \otimes B)
                    \\,.
            %p
              The oplax structure is given by the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander-Whitney+map"} Alexander-Whitney map
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Δ
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %mo ,
                        %mi B
                    %mo :
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\Delta_{A,B} : N(A \otimes B ) \to N(A) \otimes N(B)
                    \\,.
            %p The composite
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo ∇
                          %mrow
                            %mi A
                            %mo ,
                            %mi B
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi Δ
                          %mrow
                            %mi A
                            %mo ,
                            %mi B
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    N(A) \otimes N(B) \stackrel{\nabla_{A,B}}{\to}
                    N(A \otimes B)
                    \\stackrel{\Delta_{A,B}}{\to}
                    N(A) \otimes N(B)
            %p
              is the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity"} identity
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi Δ
                          %mrow
                            %mi A
                            %mo ,
                            %mi B
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mo ∇
                          %mrow
                            %mi A
                            %mo ,
                            %mi B
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    N(A \otimes B)
                    \\stackrel{\Delta_{A,B}}{\to}
                    N(A) \otimes N(B)
                    \\stackrel{\nabla_{A,B}}{\to}
                    N(A \otimes B)
            %p
              is a chain
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+equivalence"} homotopy equivalence
          %p
            Apparently the basic result (without the bilax and Frobenius structure) appears in (
            %a{:href => "#MacLaneHomology"}> MacLaneHomology
            ). The AW/EZ equivalences for the normalized chains functor are a special case of the strong deformation retract of chain complexes that was constructed in (
            %a{:href => "#EilenbergMacLane"}> EilenbergMacLane
            ). A review of the proof of symmetric lax monoidalness can be found also in section 8.5.4 of (
            %a{:href => "#Weibel"}> Weibel
            ). The bilax monoidal and Frobenius structure is described in chapter 5 of (
            %a{:href => "#AguiarMahajan"}> AguiarMahajan
            ). The Frobenius structure has also been observed independently by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kathryn+Hess"} Kathryn Hess
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Steve+Lack"} Steve Lack
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#SchwedeShipley"} SchwedeShipley
          .proof
            %h6#proof Proof
            %p
              The lax monoidal transformation that exhibits the lax-monoidalness of the Moore chain complex functor is the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/shuffle+map"} shuffle map
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∇
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %mo ,
                        %mi B
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi N
                      %mo •
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi N
                      %mo •
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi N
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\nabla_{A,B} : (N_\bullet A) \otimes (N_\bullet B)
                    \\to N_\bullet (A \otimes B)
            %p
              on a pair
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ,
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,B
              of simplicial abelian groups is the morphism of
              = succeed "es" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complex"} chain complex
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mi p
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msub
                      %mi b
                      %mi q
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mi p
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mi q
                    %mo =
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mi p
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a_p\otimes b_q \in A_p \otimes B_q =: C_p(A) \otimes C_q(B)
              to
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∇
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %mo ,
                        %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi μ
                        %mo ,
                        %mi ν
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi sign
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi μ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ν
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi s
                      %mi ν
                    %mi a
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msub
                      %mi s
                      %mi μ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                        %mo +
                        %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                        %mo +
                        %mi q
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msub
                      %mi B
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                        %mo +
                        %mi q
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\nabla_{A,B}(a \otimes b)
                    \=
                    \\sum_{(\mu,\nu)}  sign(\mu,\nu) (s_\nu a) \otimes s_\mu(b)
                    \\in
                    C_{p+q}(A \otimes B) = A_{p+q}\otimes B_{p+q}
                    \\,.
            %p
              Here the sum is over all
              = succeed "-shuffles," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi p
                      %mo ,
                      %mi q
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (p,q)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %msub
                      %mi μ
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi μ
                      %mi p
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi ν
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi ν
                      %mi q
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{\mu_1, \dots, \mu_p, \nu_1, \cdots, \nu_q\}
              of the set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ,
                    %mi p
                    %mo +
                    %mi q
                    %mo −
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{0,1,\cdots,p+q-1\}
              that leave the first
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
              and the last
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
              elements in their natural order.
            %p
              The sign in the above sum is the corresponding sign of this permutation and the degeneracy maps
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi s
                      %mi μ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s_\mu
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi s
                      %mi ν
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s_\nu
              denote the maps
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi s
                      %mi μ
                    %mo :
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi s
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi μ
                          %mi p
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ∘
                    %msub
                      %mi s
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi μ
                          %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    s_\mu := s_{\mu_p} \cdots \circ s_{\mu_1}
            %p
              and similarly for
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi s
                      %mi ν
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s_\nu
            %blockquote
              %p (Hm, is that consistent?)
          .num_cor
            %h6#corollary Corollary
            %p
              Since the normalized Moore complex functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi N
                      %mo •
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N_\bullet
              is an equivalence of categories, by
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/doctrinal+adjunction"} doctrinal adjunction
              its inverse nerve functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi Ch
                      %mo +
                    %mo →
                    %mi sAb
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma : Ch_+ \to sAb
              also acquires a lax monoidal and a oplax symmetric monoidal structure.
          %p
            For more details see
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/oplax+monoidal+functor"} oplax monoidal functor
          #RemarkOnLaxStrongDistinction.num_remark
            %h6#remark Remark
            %p
              The upshot is that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
              are both pretty close to being
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strong+monoidal+functors"} strong monoidal functors
            %p
              Explicitly, the failure of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
              to be strong monoidal is in that the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-Zilber+map"} Eilenberg-Zilber map
              is (on normalized chain complexes) a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+inverse"} right inverse
              to the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander-Whitney+map"} Alexander-Whitney map
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/left+inverse"} left inverse
              %em is
              a homotopy-inverse: because the components of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander-Whitney+map"} Alexander-Whitney map
              are (as discussed there)
              = succeed "s." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-isomorphism"} quasi-isomorphism
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+with+weak+equivalences"} 2-out-of-3
              it follows that also the EZ-maps are quasi-isomorphisms and that these are indeed inverse to the AW map in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy category
              of chain complexes (the
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/derived+category"} derived category
            %p
              Therefore we expect that the monoidal Dold-Kan correspondence holds, while not necessarily at the level of ordinary categories, at least at the level of
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopical+categories"} homotopical categories
          .num_remark
            %h6#remark_2 Remark
            %p Note that
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  the oplax structure of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi N
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
              %li
                %p
                  and the lax structure of
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Γ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            %p
              are not
              = succeed "s," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+functor"} symmetric monoidal functor
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
              structure. However, this, too, they do respect up to
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy"} homotopy
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-k-operad"} E-infinity monoidal functors
              in a suitable sense. This is shown in (
              %a{:href => "#Richter"}> Richter
              ).
          %p
            This implies that generalized
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg%E2%80%93Mac+Lane+spectra"} Eilenberg–Mac Lane spectra
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+graded+algebra"} differential graded commutative algebras
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-k+operad"} E-infinity monoids
            in the category of
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                    %mi ℤ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H \mathbb{Z}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/module"} module
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spectrum"} spectra
          %p
            The article (
            %a{:href => "#Richter"}> Richter
            ) shows that the inverse
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ξ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Xi
            from chain complexes to simplicial abelian groups sends algebras over arbitrary differential graded
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-k+operad"} E-infinity-operad
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-k-operad"} E-infinity-algebra
            in simplicial modules, and is part of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+adjunction"} Quillen adjunction
            for these.
          %h3#quillen_equivalences Quillen equivalences
          %p
            We discuss
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+equivalence"} Quillen equivalence
          %p Write
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi N
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Ch
                    %mo •
                    %mo +
                  %munderover
                    %mo ⊥
                    %munder
                      %mo ⟶
                      %mi Γ
                    %mover
                      %mo ⟵
                      %mi N
                  %mi s
                  %mi Ab
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (\Gamma \dashv N) / (N \dashv \Gamma)
                  \:
                  Ch_\bullet^+
                  \\underoverset{\underset{\Gamma}{\longrightarrow}}{\overset{N}{\longleftarrow}}{\bot}
                  s Ab
          %p
            for the ordinary
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dold-Kan+correspondence"} Dold-Kan correspondence
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Ch
                    %mo •
                    %mo +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ch_\bullet^+
            denotes the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connective+chain+complex"} connective
            chain complexes, i.e. with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential"} differential
            of degree -1 and concentrated in degrees
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \geq 0
          %p
            Since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
            are strictly inverse to each other, this may be regarded as a pair of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functor"} adjoint functor
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category"} model category
            structures (the projective
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+chain+complexes"} model structure on chain complexes
            (fibrations the degreewise surjections in positive degree) and the projective
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+simplicial+T-algebras"} model structure on simplicial abelian groups
            (fibrations the underlying
            = succeed ")s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kan+fibration"} Kan fibration
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+equivalence"} Quillen equivalence
          %p
          #MonoidalCategoryStructures.num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              %strong (Monoidal category strcutures)
              %br/
              Let
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msubsup
                          %mi Ch
                          %mo •
                          %mo +
                        %mo ,
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Ch_\bullet^+, \otimes)
                  be the standard
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
                  structure on the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+chain+complexes"} category of chain complexes
                  via the
                  = succeed ";" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+chain+complexes"} tensor product of chain complexes
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi sAb
                        %mo ,
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (sAb, \otimes)
                  be the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure"} structure
                  that is degreewise that of
                  = succeed "," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ab"} Ab
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product+of+abelian+groups"} tensor product of abelian groups
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (C, \otimes)
              a monoidal category, write
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Mon
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mon(C)
              for its
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+monoids"} category of monoids
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Mon
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msubsup
                          %mi Ch
                          %mo •
                          %mo +
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mon(Ch_\bullet^+)
                  is the category of
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connective+chain+complex"} connective
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+graded+rings"} differential graded rings
              %li
                %p
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Mon
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi sAb
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mon(sAb)
                  is the category of
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+rings"} simplicial rings
            %p
          .num_lemma
            %h6#lemma Lemma
            %p
              The DK correspondence exhibits connective dg-rings as a full
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subcategory"} subcategory
              of simplicial rings
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo :
                    %mi Mon
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Ch
                      %mo •
                      %mo +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi Mon
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi sAb
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\Gamma
                    \:
                    Mon(Ch_\bullet^+)
                    \\hookrightarrow
                    Mon(sAb)
                    \\,.
            %p The composite functor
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo :
                    %mi Mon
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Ch
                      %mo •
                      %mo +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi Mon
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Ch
                      %mo •
                      %mo +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    N \Gamma
                    \:
                    Mon(Ch_\bullet^+)
                    \\hookrightarrow
                    Mon(Ch_\bullet^+)
            %p is equivalent to the identity.
          %h4#QuillenEquSimpkAlgs
            Simplicial
            = succeed "-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              a commutative ring, there is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+equivalence"} Quillen equivalence
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi Γ
                      %mi mon
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Alg
                      %mi k
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi Δ
                          %mi op
                    %munderover
                      %mo ⊥
                      %munder
                        %mo ⟶
                        %mi N
                      %mover
                        %mo ⟵
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi Γ
                            %mi mon
                    %msub
                      %mi dgAlg
                      %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (\Gamma^{mon} \dashv N)
                    \\colon
                    Alg_k^{\Delta^{op}}
                    \\underoverset{\underset{N}{\longrightarrow}}{\overset{\Gamma^{mon}}{\longleftarrow}}{\bot}
                    dgAlg_k
            %p between
            %ul
              %li
                %p
                  the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+dg-algebras"} model structure on dg-algebras
                  for connective
                  = succeed "s" do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-algebra"} dg-algebra
                  = succeed ":" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi k
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
                  = succeed "s," do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quasi-isomorphism"} quasi-isomorphism
              %li
                %p
                  and the
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+simplicial+algebras"} model structure on simplicial algebras
                  over
                  = succeed ":" do
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mi k
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
                  = succeed "." do
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+simplicial+sets"} model structure on simplicial sets
          %p
            This appears in section 4.2 of (
            %a{:href => "#SchwedeShipley"}> SchwedeShipley
            ).
          %p
            Below in
            %a{:href => "#AlgebrasOverdgAlgebra"} Simplicial algebras and dg-Algebras
            a generalization of this statement is discussed. But it is worthwhile to spell out the proof just of this special case here.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_2 Proof
            %p
              Regard the ordinary
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dold-Kan+correspondence"} Dold-Kan correspondence
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Mod
                      %mi k
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi Δ
                          %mi op
                    %munderover
                      %mo ⊥
                      %munder
                        %mo ⟶
                        %mi N
                      %mover
                        %mo ⟵
                        %mi Γ
                    %msub
                      %mi Ch
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi k
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo +
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (\Gamma \dashv N)
                    \:
                    Mod_k^{\Delta^{op}}
                    \\underoverset{\underset{N}{\longrightarrow}}{\overset{\Gamma}{\longleftarrow}}{\bot}
                    Ch_\bullet(k)^+
            %p
              as a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+equivalence"} Quillen equivalence
              between the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+simplicial+T-algebras"} model structure on simplicial k-modules
              and the projective
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+chain+complexes"} model structure on chain complexes
              (fibrations the degreewise surjections in positive degree) with
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
              regarded as the
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/right+adjoint"} right adjoint
              \. Notice that both
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
              preserve
              %em all
              weak equivalences and that the unit and counit of the adjunction are
              = succeed "s." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            %p
              Both model categories involved are
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+model+categories"} monoidal model categories
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+Quillen+adjunction"} monoidal Quillen adjunction
              with respect to this structure.
            %p
              First of all
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax+monoidal+functor"} lax monoidal functor
              with the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax+monoidal+transformation"} lax monoidal transformation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mo ∇
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %mo ,
                        %mi B
                    %mo :
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi B
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla_{A,B} : N(A) \otimes N(B) \to N(A \otimes B)
              given by the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-Zilber+map"} Eilenberg-Zilber map
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/oplax+monoidal+functor"} oplax monoidal functor
              this induces an oplax monoidal structure on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
              given by the composite
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Δ
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                        %mo ,
                        %mi Y
                    %mo :
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %mi Γ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi i
                          %mi X
                        %mo ⊗
                        %msub
                          %mi i
                          %mi Y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi N
                    %mi Γ
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi N
                    %mi Γ
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %mi Γ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mo ∇
                          %mrow
                            %mi Γ
                            %mi X
                            %mo ,
                            %mi Γ
                            %mi Y
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi Γ
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Γ
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi Γ
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi ϵ
                          %mrow
                            %mi Γ
                            %mi X
                            %mo ⊗
                            %mi Γ
                            %mi Y
                    %mi Γ
                    %mi X
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi Γ
                    %mi Y
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\Delta_{X,Y}
                    \:
                    \\Gamma(X \otimes Y) \stackrel{\Gamma(i_X \otimes i_Y)}{\to}
                    \\Gamma(N \Gamma X \otimes N \Gamma Y)
                    \\stackrel{\Gamma(\nabla_{\Gamma X, \Gamma Y})}{\to}
                    \\Gamma N(\Gamma X \otimes \Gamma Y)
                    \\stackrel{\epsilon_{\Gamma X \otimes \Gamma Y}}{\to}
                    \\Gamma X \otimes \Gamma Y
                    \\,.
            %p
              Now the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-Zilber+map"} Eilenberg-Zilber map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∇
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
              is (as discussed there) a chain
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+equivalence"} homotopy equivalence
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi i
                      %mo ⋅
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i_\cdot
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ϵ
                      %mo ⋅
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon_\cdot
              are even isomorphisms. Since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Γ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
              preserves all weak equivalences, it follows that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Δ
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                        %mo ,
                        %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta_{X,Y}
              is a weak equivalence.
            %p
              This shows that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\Gamma \dashv N)
              is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+Quillen+equivalence"} monoidal Quillen equivalence
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transferred+model+structure"} transferred model structure
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Mon
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi Ch
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi g
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msubsup
                      %mi dgAlg
                      %mi k
                      %mo +
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mon(Ch_\bullet(g)^+) = dgAlg_k^+
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Mon
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Mod
                      %mi k
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi Δ
                          %mi op
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Alg
                      %mi k
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi Δ
                          %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mon(Mod_k^{\Delta^{op}}) Alg_k^{\Delta^{op}}
              exist as indicated.
            %p
              Therefore with the
              %a{:href => "monoidal+Quillen+adjunction#LiftToMonoids"} theorem on lifts to monoids
              described at
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+Quillen+adjunction"} monoidal Quillen adjunction
              the claim follows.
          %p
            But not only is
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Γ
                  %mo ⊣
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\Gamma \dashv N)
            but also
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi N
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (N \dashv \Gamma)
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_3 Proposition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              a commutative ring, there is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+equivalence"} Quillen equivalence
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi N
                      %mi mon
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mi dgAlg
                      %mi k
                    %munderover
                      %mo ⊥
                      %munder
                        %mo ⟶
                        %mi Γ
                      %mover
                        %mo ⟵
                        %mrow
                          %msup
                            %mi N
                            %mi mon
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Alg
                      %mi k
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi Δ
                          %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (N^{mon} \dashv \Gamma)
                    \\colon
                    dgAlg_k
                    \\underoverset{\underset{\Gamma}{\longrightarrow}}{\overset{N^{mon}}{\longleftarrow}}{\bot}
                    Alg_k^{\Delta^{op}}
            %p for the above model structures.
          .proof
            %h6#proof_3 Proof
            %p We check that
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi N
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mi Γ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Ch
                      %mo •
                      %mo +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %munderover
                      %mo ⊥
                      %munder
                        %mo ⟶
                        %mi Γ
                      %mover
                        %mo ⟵
                        %mi N
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Mod
                      %mi k
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi Δ
                          %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (N \dashv \Gamma)
                    \\colon
                    Ch_\bullet^+(k)
                    \\underoverset{\underset{\Gamma}{\longrightarrow}}{\overset{N}{\longleftarrow}}{\bot}
                    Mod_k^{\Delta^{op}}
            %p
              is a
              = succeed ":" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+Quillen+adjunction"} monoidal Quillen adjunction
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
              is given by the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander-Whitney+map"} Alexander-Whitney map
            %p
              Therefore with the
              %a{:href => "monoidal+Quillen+adjunction#LiftToMonoids"} theorem on lifts to monoids
              described at
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+Quillen+adjunction"} monoidal Quillen adjunction
              the claim follows.
          %h4#AlgebrasOverdgAlgebra
            Simplicial
            = succeed "-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi A
                      %mo •
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_\bullet
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              a commutative
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+ring"} simplicial ring
              there is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+equivalence"} Quillen equivalence
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Q
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi Alg
                      %mi A
                    %munderover
                      %mo ⊥
                      %munder
                        %mo ⟶
                        %mi N
                      %mover
                        %mo ⟵
                        %mi Q
                    %msub
                      %mi Alg
                      %mrow
                        %mi N
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi A
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (Q \dashv N) : Alg_A \underoverset{\underset{N}{\longrightarrow}}{\overset{Q}{\longleftarrow}}{\bot} Alg_{N(A)}
            %p
              between simplicial
              = succeed "-algebras" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
              = succeed "-algebras," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi N
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N(A)
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Γ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Gamma
          %p
            This is the main theorem in (
            %a{:href => "#SchwedeShipley"}> SchwedeShipley
            ).
          %h4#commutative_and_algebras
            Commutative and
            = succeed "-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_\infty
          %p
            Notice that the above statement is
            %em not
            formulated for commutative monoids. But
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_2 Theorem
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/characteristic"} characteristic
              0 there is a Quillen equivalence
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Q
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mi N
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msubsup
                      %mi sAlg
                      %mi k
                      %mi C
                    %munderover
                      %mo ⊥
                      %munder
                        %mo ⟶
                        %mi N
                      %mover
                        %mo ⟵
                        %mi Q
                    %msubsup
                      %mi dgAlg
                      %mi k
                      %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    (Q \dashv N)
                    \\colon
                    sAlg^C_k
                    \\underoverset{\underset{N}{\longrightarrow}}{\overset{Q}{\longleftarrow}}{\bot}
                    dgAlg^C_k
            %p
              between connected commutative connective
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-algebra"} dg-algebra
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              and connected commutative
              = succeed "s" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+algebra"} simplicial algebra
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %p
              Here connected means: trivial in degree
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo ≤
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \leq 0
              (= “reduced”).
          %p
            This was originally mentioned in (
            %a{:href => "#Quillen69"}> Quillen 69, remark on p. 223
            ) as following in a way analogous to the Quillen equivalence between
            = succeed "-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
            %a{:href => "#Fresse"} Fresse, 6.4.1a, 6.4.5
          %p In arbitrary characteristic we have instead
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_3 Theorem
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              a commutative ring, there is a Quillen equivalence
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi dgAlg
                      %mi k
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi E
                          %mn ∞
                    %mover
                      %munder
                        %mo →
                        %mrow
                      %mover
                        %mo ←
                        %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi sAlg
                      %mi k
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi E
                          %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    dgAlg^{E_\infty}_k \stackrel{\overset{}{\leftarrow}}{\underset{}{\to}}  sAlg^{E_\infty}_k
            %p
              between connective dg-
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-%E2%88%9E+algebra"} E-∞ algebra
              over
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              and simplicial
              = succeed "-algebras" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi E
                        %mn ∞
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_\infty
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
          %p
            This is in (
            %a{:href => "#Mandell"}> Mandell
            ).
          %p
            Here the model category structures are analogous to those before: for simplicial
            = succeed "-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_\infty
            = succeed "-algebras" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi E
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} E_\infty
          %h4#module_spectra_and_unbounded_dgalgebras
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mi ℤ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H \mathbb{Z}
            \-module spectra and unbounded dg-algebras
          %p An unbounded (“stable”) analog of the monoidal Dold-Kan correspondence is:
          %p
            there is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+equivalence"} Quillen equivalence
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi H
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mi Alg
                  %mo ≃
                  %msub
                    %mi dgAlg
                    %mi ℤ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  H \mathbb{Z} Alg \simeq dgAlg_{\mathbb{Z}}
          %p
            between the model structure on
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Eilenberg-MacLane+spectrum"} Eilenberg-MacLane spectrum
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+spectra"} algebra spectra
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+dg-algebras"} model structure on dg-algebras
            (unbounded). See
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+spectrum"} algebra spectrum
            for more on this.
          %h2#cosimplicial Cosimplicial algebras and cochain dg-algebras
          %p
            The monoidal Dold-Kan correspondence relating cosimplicial algebras to cochain dg-algebras is considered less prominently explicitly in the literature, but does appear implicitly in much classical work. For instance the classical statement that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cochains+on+simplicial+sets"} cochains on simplicial sets
            form a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-algebra"} dg-algebra
            that is commutative up to coherent higher homotopy, i.e. that is an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/E-k-operad"} E-infinity algebra
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
            = succeed "-monoidal" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %p
            One article that does make a cosimplicial/cochain monoidal Dold-Kan correspondence explicit is (
            %a{:href => "#CastiglioniCortinas"}> CastiglioniCortinas
            )
          %p
            This establishes not quite a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+equivalence"} Quillen equivalence
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dold-Kan+correspondence"} Dold-Kan correspondence
            induces an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence"} equivalence
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+category"} homotopy categories
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+cosimplicial+rings"} model structure on cosimplicial rings
            and the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+structure+on+dg-algebras"} model structure on dg-rings
          %p
            However, this article explicitly constructs the (derived)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+functor"} adjoint functor
            to the Moore cochain complex functor.
          %p
            Explicit discussion of the Moore co-chain complex functor as inducing an
            = succeed "-monoidal" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %h3#lax_and_oplax_monoidalness_of_conormalized_cochains Lax and oplax monoidalness of conormalized cochains
          %h4#AlexWhitneyShuffle Alexander–Whitney and shuffle morphisms
          %p A central ingredient in the monoidal Dold-Kan correspondence are the Alexander–Whitney and the shuffle morphisms.
          %p See chapter VI, paragraph 12 of
          %ul
            %li
              A. Dold,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Lectures on algebraic topology
          %p or chapter VIII, paragraph 8 of
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Saunders+MacLane"}> Saunders MacLane
              ,
              %em Homology
              , Grundlehren Math. Wiss. vol 114, Springer-Verlag, Berlin-Göttingen-Heidelberg, 1963 .
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A, B
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosimplicial+abelian+groups"} cosimplicial abelian groups
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C A
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C B
            their
            = succeed "es," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Moore+complex"} Moore cochain complex
          %p
            the
            %strong Alexander–Whitney morphism
            is the morphism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi N
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  N A \otimes N B \to N(A \otimes B)
          %p
            that is given on homogeneous elements
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x, y
            of degree
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi p
                    %mo ,
                    %mi q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p,q
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi y
                  %mo ↦
                  %msup
                    %mi δ
                    %mi n
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ∘
                  %msup
                    %mi δ
                    %mrow
                      %mi p
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msup
                    %mi δ
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ∘
                  %msup
                    %mi δ
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  x \otimes y \mapsto
                  \\delta^n \circ \cdots \circ \delta^{p+1}(x)
                  \\;\;\otimes
                  \\;\;
                  \\delta^0 \circ \cdots \circ \delta^0
                  (y)
                  \\,.
          %p
            Notice that for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo =
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A = B
            a cosimplicial algebra, further composing this with the product yields the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cup+product"} cup product
            induced on dg-algebras of cosimplicial algebras. This is spelled out in detail below.
          %p
            The
            %strong shuffle morphism
            goes the other way
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi C
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  C(A \otimes B) \to C A \otimes C B
          %p and is given on homogeneous elements as above by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi y
                  %mo ↦
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi μ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi ν
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi μ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ν
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msup
                    %mi σ
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ν
                        %mn 1
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ∘
                  %msup
                    %mi σ
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi ν
                        %mi q
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msup
                    %mi σ
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi μ
                        %mn 1
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ∘
                  %msup
                    %mi σ
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi μ
                        %mi p
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  x  \otimes y
                  \\mapsto
                  \\sum_{(\mu,\nu)}
                  \\epsilon(\mu,\nu)
                  \\sigma^{\nu_1 - 1}
                  \\circ \cdots
                  \\circ
                  \\sigma^{\nu_q - 1}
                  (x)
                  \\;\; \otimes
                  \\;\;
                  \\sigma^{\mu_1 - 1} \circ \cdots \circ
                  \\sigma^{\mu_p - 1}(y)
          %p
            where the sum is over all
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi p
                  %mo ,
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (p,q)
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/shuffle"} shuffle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi μ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ν
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\mu,\nu)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϵ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi μ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ν
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \epsilon(\mu,\nu)
            is the sign of the shuffle.
          %p
            Both the Alexander–Whitney morphism as well as the shuffle morphism respect the passage to the normalized
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Moore+complex"} Moore complex
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N A
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C A
            and hence induce also morphisms
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi AW
                  %mo :
                  %mi N
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi N
                  %mi B
                  %mo →
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  AW : N A \otimes N B \to N(A \otimes B)
          %p and
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo :
                  %mi N
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi B
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi N
                  %mi A
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi N
                  %mi B
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  S : N(A \otimes B) \to N A \otimes N B
                  \\,.
          %p In this form they satisfy
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi AW
                  %mo =
                  %mi Id
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  S \circ AW  = Id
                  \\,.
          %p
            See for instance theorem 2.1.a in (
            %a{:href => "#EilenbergMacLane"}> EilenbergMacLane
            ).
          %p A quick summary of all this is in section 7 of
          %ul
            %li
              José Burgos Gil,
              %em The regulators of Beilinson and Borel
              (
              %a{:href => "http://atlas.mat.ub.es/personals/burgos/files/brbr.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %h4#lax_monoidalness_of_the_moore_cochain_complex_functor Lax monoidalness of the Moore co-chain complex functor
          .standout
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Urs+Schreiber"}> Urs Schreiber
              \:
            %p
              We claim to show that the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Moore+complex"} Moore cochain complex functor
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo :
                    %mi CoS
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Ab
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %msubsup
                      %mi Ch
                      %mo +
                      %mo •
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Ab
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    C : CoS(Ab) \to Ch_+^\bullet(Ab)
            %p
              from
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosimplicial+abelian+groups"} cosimplicial abelian groups
              to cochain complexes is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax+monoidal+functor"} lax monoidal functor
              with respect to the standard monoidal structures on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi CoS
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Ab
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CoS(Ab)
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi Ch
                        %mo +
                        %mo •
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Ab
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ch_+^\bullet(Ab)
            %p
              This should be old and standard, but somehow explicit statements in the literature to this extent are hard to find.(?) Some central aspects are recalled in
              %a{:href => "http://atlas.mat.ub.es/personals/burgos/files/brbr.pdf#page=63"} section 7
              of
            %ul
              %li
                José Burgos Gil,
                %em The regulators of Beilinson and Borel
                (
                %a{:href => "http://atlas.mat.ub.es/personals/burgos/files/brbr.pdf"}> pdf
                )
          %p
            Every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi CoS
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi An
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CoS(An)
            – a cosimplicial ring – the Moore cochain complex
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(K)
            is naturally equipped with the structure of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Ch
                    %mo +
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Ab
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ch_+^\bullet(Ab)
            – a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+graded+algebra"} differential graded algebra
            – by letting the product
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⌣
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \smile : C(K)\otimes C(K) \to C(K)
            be given by the composite
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⌣
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi μ
                        %mrow
                          %mi K
                          %mo ,
                          %mi K
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi K
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\smile : C(K)\otimes C(K) \stackrel{\mu_{K,K}}{\to} C(K \otimes K) \stackrel{C(-\cdot -)}{\to} C(K)
          %p where
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi μ
                        %mrow
                          %mi K
                          %mo ,
                          %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu_{K,K}
                is the component of the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax+monoidal+transformation"} lax monoidal transformation
                that exhibits the lax monoidal structure (the “compositor”);
            %li
              %p
                The operation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo ⋅
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo :
                      %mi K
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi K
                      %mo →
                      %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} -\cdot - : K \otimes K \to K
                is the product on
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi K
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
          .standout
            %p
              This monoidal structure induced from a cosimplicial ring
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
              on its Moore cochain complex on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(K)
              is the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cup+product"} cup product
          %p
            More precisely, for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi Δ
                        %mi Top
                        %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^{\Delta^n_{Top}}
            the set of
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplex"} simplices
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+set"} simplicial set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Π
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %msubsup
                        %mi Δ
                        %mi Top
                        %mo •
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Pi(X) = X^{\Delta^\bullet_{Top}}
            – the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fundamental+%E2%88%9E-groupoid"} fundamental ∞-groupoid
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            – and for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
            let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Maps
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Π
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Maps(\Pi(X),R)
            be the cosimplicial ring of maps
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mrow
                        %msubsup
                          %mi Δ
                          %mi Top
                          %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^{\Delta^n_{Top}} \to R
          %p Then
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the Moore cochain complex
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Maps
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Π
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mi R
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(Maps(\Pi(X),R))
                is the cochain complex that computes the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+cohomology"} singular cohomology
                of
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %li
              %p
                the monoid structure induced on
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Maps
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Π
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ,
                      %mi R
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(Maps(\Pi(X),R))
                by the lax monoidalness of the Moore cochain complex functor is the familiar
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cup+product"} cup product
                on
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+cohomology"} singular cohomology
          %p We now derive this in detail.
          .standout
            %p Check.
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            some
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
            write
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi CoS
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CoS(A)
            for the category of
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+object"} cosimplicial object
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Ch
                    %mo +
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Ab
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ch_+^\bullet(Ab)
            for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+chain+complexes"} category of cochain complexes
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            concentrated in non-negative degree – called connective or
            = succeed "-graded" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℕ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{N}
          %p
            Recall that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Moore+complex"} Moore cochain complex
            functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo :
                  %mi CoS
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Ch
                    %mo +
                    %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  C : CoS(A) \to Ch_+^\bullet(A)
          %p
            is an
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalence of categories
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dold-Kan+correspondence"} Dold-Kan correspondence
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
